<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title><%= @title ? "#{h(@title)} &mdash; Go Links" : 'Go Links' %></title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/main.css">
  <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Ccircle cx='16' cy='16' r='13' fill='%2363b37b'/%3E%3C/svg%3E">
  <%# Blocking, in <head>, and not a module: this has to set the theme before
      the first paint or the page flashes the system theme and then corrects.
      Only the read-and-apply is here; the toggle button's wiring defers to
      /js/theme-toggle.js, which is not on this page's critical path. %>
  <script>
    try {
      const stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
      if (stored === 'light' || stored === 'dark') document.documentElement.dataset.theme = stored;
    } catch {
      /* localStorage throws in some privacy modes; the system theme is the default anyway. */
    }
  </script>

  <%# A nested import is invisible to the preload scanner: the browser only
      learns the Fuse URL after links.js has been fetched and parsed, so the
      DNS, TCP and TLS work would otherwise all start late. %>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net" crossorigin>
</head>
<body>
  <%# First thing on every page, above even the h1: until these two steps are done,
      nothing the app does is reachable from the address bar, which makes this the
      only thing on screen that matters on a fresh install. Once dismissed it's gone,
      so it costs the rest of the app nothing thereafter — which is also why it lives
      here rather than on the list alone. Setting up the shortcut is about the app,
      not about one page, and a reader who lands on the add-link form first shouldn't
      have to find their way to the list to be told.

      Outside <main> deliberately: it's a standing notice about the app, not the
      content of whichever page you're on. Body-level, so it takes the same column.

      Shown to every browser. The steps describe Chrome's settings screen, so they're
      only actionable in Chrome — but that's a judgement about whether they're useful
      to you, not something the server should be making on your behalf from a
      User-Agent string. The dismiss button is the answer for anyone they don't apply
      to, and it's a better one than a sniff that has to keep pace with which
      Chromium browsers do and don't share that screen.

      tabindex="-1" so restoring it from the footer can move focus here — not a tab
      stop, just a focus target (see js/setup-callout.js). %>
  <aside class="setup-callout" data-setup-callout tabindex="-1" hidden>
    <button type="button" class="setup-dismiss" data-setup-dismiss aria-label="Dismiss setup instructions">&times;</button>
    <h2 class="setup-title">Activate Go Links in Chrome</h2>
    <p class="setup-lead">To activate Go Links:</p>
    <%# A real <ol>: these are a sequence, and the second step is meaningless
        before the first, so the numbers carry information rather than decorate. %>
    <ol class="setup-steps">
      <li>
        Navigate to <strong>Chrome settings &gt; Search engine &gt; Manage search engines and site search &gt; Site search</strong>
        or paste <%= copyable('chrome://settings/searchEngines') %> into address bar.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add a site search entry with name <%= copyable('Go Links') %>,
        shortcut <%= copyable('go') %> and URL <%= copyable("http://localhost:#{PORT}/%s") %>.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </aside>
  <%# Unhidden here rather than from the deferred module: a module runs after first
      paint, so the callout would appear a beat late and shove the whole page down
      under the pointer. Only the read-and-unhide is inline — copy, dismiss and
      restore wire up in the modules at the end of the body. Same split as the theme
      script above, for the same reason. %>
  <script>
    try {
      if (!localStorage.getItem('setupDismissed')) {
        document.querySelector('[data-setup-callout]').hidden = false;
      }
    } catch {
      /* localStorage throws in some privacy modes; showing the callout is the right default. */
      document.querySelector('[data-setup-callout]').hidden = false;
    }
  </script>

  <main><%= yield %></main>

  <%# Belongs to js/copyable.js, not to the callout that happens to hold today's only
      copy chips — so it sits outside the <aside>, where dismissing the callout can't
      take it with it. position: fixed, so where it is in the document doesn't affect
      where it lands. Doubles as the live region: the confirmation a mouse user sees
      float next to the cursor is the same one a screen reader announces. %>
  <span class="copied-label" data-copied-label role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>

  <footer class="page-footer">
    <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>localhost:<%= PORT %></p>
    <div class="footer-actions">
      <%# Always visible, whether or not the callout is currently up — a control that
          appears only after you dismiss something is one you have to have noticed to
          know about, and this is the standing answer to "how do I set this up
          again?". It sits next to the callout it reaches, on every page, so neither
          needs a flag from the route. %>
      <button type="button" class="theme-toggle setup-restore" data-setup-restore>setup instructions</button>
      <%# Label and aria-label are filled in by js/theme-toggle.js, which is the
          only thing that knows the current mode. %>
      <button type="button" class="theme-toggle" data-theme-toggle></button>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script type="module" src="/js/theme-toggle.js"></script>
  <script type="module" src="/js/copyable.js"></script>
  <script type="module" src="/js/setup-callout.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
